Draw the **Lattice source**, a 6-frame **sprite sheet** of a top-down test-fixture
item emitter, using only the drawing tool.

The source is a renderer sprite for **Lattice**, a flat top-down factory
simulation. It is a **test fixture**, not a normal factory machine: it emits a
single item onto its output belt once every fixed period, the deterministic way
items enter the factory. You are drawing one tile of the source that **emits
toward the right (East)** — its output aperture is on the East edge, with a green
status indicator, and the six frames are an emit pulse where the indicator and
aperture brighten as the fixture fires, then dim, looping seamlessly. Draw the
**fixture only — no item**: the renderer draws the actual item being emitted, so a
fixed item baked into the sprite would wrongly show the same cargo every time. The
renderer rotates this one sprite for the other directions, so you draw the
East-facing source only.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`. You
build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a
time**, each targeting a frame with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you
issue are recorded to a per-frame action log, and that log is your submission.

Each frame is its **own separate 32×32 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-rect --frame 0 --x 4 --y 6 --width 22 --height 20 --color "#4d5a64"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and
decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the source sprite sheet up operation by operation until each
`frames/<index>.png` matches the brief: a flat top-down test-fixture emitter
housing with a green status indicator and an East-facing output aperture, the six
frames pulsing the indicator and aperture bright as the fixture fires then back to
idle — no item drawn — so frames 0→5 loop seamlessly. When you are satisfied,
stop: the recorded per-frame action logs are your finished submission.
